"""Route to read-only memories by asking Jev, then move the acting memory the language model serves most into a decision program.

The router is written by the writer from the contract. It asks Jev whether a request asks for one memory's work to be
done now, and serves a read-only memory otherwise. It is kept only if practice shows it is better than plain recall.
Then the acting memory the language model served most often is rewritten as a decision program. That program is kept
only if practice shows no new wrong actions and no loss.
"""

KINDS_PROMPT = """{contract}

Below are runs where the assistant, a language model, handled requests for
this job: each request, every tool call with its result, and the verdict.
Some requests are worded oddly, some should not be acted on at all, and
some runs went wrong.

List the kinds of request these runs show, and for each write what the
assistant should know the next time it meets one: how ids are really
written in the tools, which tools to call and in what order, and the
mistakes to avoid. Use as few kinds as cover the runs. Include one kind for
requests that should not be acted on.

Reply with only a JSON list. Each item has the keys "name" (short, in
snake_case), "description" (which requests belong to this kind, and which
similar ones don't), "guidance" (what the assistant should know), and
"acts" (true if a request of this kind should end in an action, false if
it should only be answered or passed on).

{episodes}"""

ROUTER = """async def decide(request, kit):
    found = await kit.recall(request, k=1)
    if len(found) == 0:
        return {"handoff": "nothing learned applies"}
    return {"serve": found[0]["name"]}
"""

ROUTER_PROMPT = """{contract}

You are writing the program that picks a memory for each request. These are the memories (name, route, read_only, description):
{memories}

The current router only recalls the closest memory and serves it. That fails badly: requests that merely resemble an acting
memory's work (questions about it, status checks, refusals such as "don't do X", holds, changes, moves, undoing, reminders,
rejections, asking who or when) get served by an acting memory and are left undone. Such requests must go read-only.

Write a new `async def decide(request, kit)` that:
- calls `await kit.recall(request, k=4)` to find candidate memories;
- asks Jev, exactly as the contract above describes, for a typed judgment: does the request ask, right now, for the work one
  of the acting memories (read_only false) describes to be carried out, and which one? Give Jev the acting memories' names
  and descriptions in the question. Anything else (a question, a status check, a refusal, a hold, a change or undo,
  a reminder, a rejection, a different job) is not that work;
- if the request asks for an acting memory's work, returns {{"serve": that name}};
- otherwise returns {{"serve": "{readonly}"}}, which is a read-only memory;
- if Jev fails or its answer is not one of the names, serves "{readonly}".

Never look for words in the request; every judgment about what it asks comes from Jev.
Use no imports and no names starting with an underscore. Use no bare except and no str.format. Use only these builtins: Exception, KeyError,
TypeError, ValueError, abs, all, any, bool, dict, divmod, enumerate, filter, float, int, isinstance, len, list, map, max,
min, range, reversed, round, set, sorted, str, sum, tuple, zip.
Reply with only the Python source.
{feedback}"""

PROGRAM_PROMPT = """{contract}

Write a decision program, `async def decide(request, kit)`, that serves requests of this kind without a language model:

name: {name}
description: {description}
what was learned: {guidance}

Below are runs a language model did for this job, with every tool call and result and the verdict. Find the runs of this
kind and do exactly what the successful ones did: the same tools, in the same order, with arguments built the same way.
Use code for what code knows exactly: find the id in the request, normalize it to how the tools write it (a request may
say "invoice 201" or "document 202" for an id the tools write differently), and call tools. When a request names
several ids, ask Jev which one the work is for. Ask Jev, as the contract describes, for anything about what the request
means. Before acting, ask Jev whether the request really asks for this work to be done now. If it does not, or anything
is unclear or a tool fails, hand off as the contract says rather than guess; a wrong action is far worse than a hand-off.
Return the action or hand-off in the form the contract gives.

Use no imports and no names starting with an underscore. Use no bare except and no str.format. Use only these builtins: Exception, KeyError,
TypeError, ValueError, abs, all, any, bool, dict, divmod, enumerate, filter, float, int, isinstance, len, list, map, max,
min, range, reversed, round, set, sorted, str, sum, tuple, zip.
Reply with only the Python source.
{feedback}

{episodes}"""

FALLBACK_NAME = "outside_the_job"
FALLBACK_DESCRIPTION = "Requests that should not be acted on: questions, status checks, refusals, holds, changes, undoing, reminders, or anything outside the job."
FALLBACK_BODY = "Do not act. Answer from what you can read, or pass the request on."


def extract(reply):
    text = reply
    start = text.find("async def decide")
    if start < 0:
        return ""
    text = text[start:]
    end = text.find("```")
    if end >= 0:
        text = text[:end]
    return text.strip() + "\n"


def describe(memories):
    lines = []
    for m in memories:
        lines.append("- " + m["name"] + " (" + str(m["route"]) + ", read_only " + str(m["read_only"]) + "): " + m["description"])
    return "\n".join(lines)


def test_ids(lab):
    if len(lab.tests) > 0:
        return [t["id"] for t in lab.tests]
    return list(lab.stream())[:30]


async def evaluate(lab, source, ids):
    total = 0.0
    zeros = 0
    rows = []
    for tid in ids:
        try:
            reward, reason, name = await lab.practice(source, tid)
        except (Exception) as err:
            reward, reason, name = 0.0, "error: " + str(err), None
        total = total + reward
        if reward <= 0:
            zeros = zeros + 1
        rows.append((tid, reward, reason, name))
    return total, zeros, rows


def failures(lab, rows):
    lines = []
    for tid, reward, reason, name in rows:
        if reward < 0.6:
            lines.append("- " + str(lab.request(tid)) + " -> reward " + str(reward) + ", memory " + str(name) + ": " + str(reason))
    return "\n".join(lines[:15])


async def learn(lab):
    kept = []
    if len(lab.memories) > 0:
        for memory in lab.memories:
            kept.append(dict(memory))
    else:
        shown = "\n\n".join(lab.render(e) for e in lab.episodes)
        reply = await lab.ask(lab.fill(KINDS_PROMPT, contract=lab.contract, episodes=shown))
        for kind in lab.parse(reply):
            kept.append({"name": kind["name"], "description": kind["description"], "body": kind["guidance"],
                         "route": "none", "read_only": not kind["acts"]})
    names = [m["name"] for m in kept]
    if FALLBACK_NAME not in names:
        kept.append({"name": FALLBACK_NAME, "description": FALLBACK_DESCRIPTION, "body": FALLBACK_BODY,
                     "route": "none", "read_only": True})
    for memory in kept:
        lab.remember(**memory)

    ids = test_ids(lab)
    best = ROUTER
    best_total, best_zeros, best_rows = await evaluate(lab, ROUTER, ids)

    feedback = ""
    for attempt in range(2):
        reply = await lab.ask(lab.fill(ROUTER_PROMPT, contract=lab.contract, memories=describe(kept),
                                       readonly=FALLBACK_NAME, feedback=feedback))
        source = extract(reply)
        if source == "":
            continue
        total, zeros, rows = await evaluate(lab, source, ids)
        if total > best_total and zeros <= best_zeros:
            best, best_total, best_zeros, best_rows = source, total, zeros, rows
            break
        feedback = "Your previous router did worse on these requests:\n" + failures(lab, rows) + "\nPrevious source:\n" + source
    lab.note("router reward " + str(best_total) + " over " + str(len(ids)))

    counts = {}
    for tid, reward, reason, name in best_rows:
        if name is not None and reward > 0.5 and reward < 0.9:
            counts[name] = counts.get(name, 0) + 1
    target = None
    for memory in kept:
        if memory["read_only"] or memory["route"] == "program":
            continue
        if memory["name"] in counts:
            if target is None or counts[memory["name"]] > counts[target["name"]]:
                target = memory
    if target is None:
        return best

    shown = "\n\n".join(lab.render(e) for e in lab.episodes)
    feedback = ""
    for attempt in range(2):
        reply = await lab.ask(lab.fill(PROGRAM_PROMPT, contract=lab.contract, name=target["name"],
                                       description=target["description"], guidance=target["body"],
                                       feedback=feedback, episodes=shown))
        program = extract(reply)
        if program == "":
            continue
        lab.remember(target["name"], target["description"], program, route="program", read_only=False)
        total, zeros, rows = await evaluate(lab, best, ids)
        if total >= best_total and zeros <= best_zeros:
            lab.note("moved " + target["name"] + " to a program: " + str(total))
            return best
        lab.remember(**target)
        feedback = "Your previous program failed on these requests:\n" + failures(lab, rows) + "\nPrevious source:\n" + program
    return best

