"""Fix routing in code, then move the acting memories with the largest gap to decision programs, worst first.

The parent's router sent requests that Jev judged outside every acting memory ("none") to the first acting memory,
so they were handed off with tools. Its whole-week planning memory was served by the language model and failed on
every planning request. This variant does three things. It builds the router from code, so it costs no writer call.
That router gives Jev the job as the acting memories describe it, and it serves read-only when Jev picks none. Then
it ranks the acting memories by the gap they leave on practice and rewrites the worst ones as decision programs. A
program has Jev extract the request into structured data, and code solves and verifies it before acting. Each program
is kept only if practice shows no new wrong action and a gain.
"""

KINDS_PROMPT = """{contract}

Below are runs where the assistant, a language model, handled requests for
this job: each request, every tool call with its result, and the verdict.
Some requests are worded oddly, some should not be acted on at all, and
some runs went wrong.

List the kinds of request these runs show, and for each write what the
assistant should know the next time it meets one: how ids are really
written in the tools, which tools to call and in what order, and the
mistakes to avoid. Use as few kinds as cover the runs. Include one kind for
requests that must not be acted on: questions, previews, hypotheticals,
reminders, undo or hold requests, things already done, and anything outside
the job.

Reply with only a JSON list. Each item has the keys "name" (short, in
snake_case), "description" (which requests belong to this kind, and which
similar ones don't), "guidance" (what the assistant should know), and
"acts" (true if a request of this kind should end in an action, false if
it should only be answered or passed on).

{episodes}"""

PROGRAM_PROMPT = """You write a decision program that serves one kind of request with no language model.

{contract}

The kind of request: {name}
Which requests belong to it: {description}
What is known about serving it:
{guidance}

When a language model served requests of this kind, this happened (request, reward, reason):
{failures}

Runs where a language model served requests for this job, with every tool call and result:
{episodes}

Write `async def decide(request, kit)` following the contract above exactly. It serves a
request of this kind by calling the job's tools and procedures, as the runs show.

How to do it well:
- Ask Jev, in typed judgments, for what the request asks for, such as who, which item, which day,
  or the full list of requirements as structured records (one record per requirement, with
  its type and its fields). Never look for words in the request text.
- Read the current state and the standing rules from the tools. The reasons above show which
  rules a language model broke, so take care to honor them.
- If the work is a planning or assignment problem, do not guess. Solve it in code. Search
  exhaustively over the possible assignments, with backtracking and small domains, until one
  meets every extracted requirement and every standing rule. Check each requirement in code
  before submitting. If a tool can validate a plan, call it before publishing.
- Verify everything Jev gives against what the tools list, such as names, ids and days.
- If anything is uncertain or unexpected, if no solution exists, or if the request turns out
  not to ask for this change, return a hand-off as the contract says instead of acting. A
  wrong action is far worse than a hand-off.
Use only these builtins: Exception, KeyError, TypeError, ValueError, abs, all, any, bool,
dict, divmod, enumerate, filter, float, int, isinstance, len, list, map, max, min, range,
reversed, round, set, sorted, str, sum, tuple, zip. Do not import anything. Do not use names
starting with an underscore, bare except, or str.format.

Reply with only the program source."""

REPAIR_PROMPT = """{contract}

This decision program was tried on requests and did not do well enough:

{source}

What happened on each request (request, reward, reason, memory that served it). A reward of 1
is good, 0.6 means the language model served it, 0.25 or less means it was not done, and 0 is a
wrong action:
{report}

Write a corrected `async def decide(request, kit)`. Keep the same rules. Decide what a request
asks for only by asking Jev, never by looking for words. Solve any planning in code with exhaustive
search and check every requirement before acting. Hand off when unsure. Use only the allowed
builtins. Do not import anything, use no names starting with an underscore, no bare except, and
no str.format. Reply with only the program source."""

ROUTER = """async def decide(request, kit):
    found = await kit.recall(request, 1)
    if len(found) == 0:
        return {"handoff": "nothing learned applies"}
    return {"serve": found[0]["name"]}
"""

ROUTER_BODY = """
async def decide(request, kit):
    try:
        check = await kit.ask(
            {"request": request, "job": JOB},
            {"change": {"type": "noul", "instructions": "The job is described in 'job': it lists the only kinds of change this job does. Does this request ask, plainly and right now, for one of those changes? Say no for questions about the current state, previews or plans that should not be published, hypotheticals, reminders for later, requests to hold off, undo or wait, things the user says they already did, and anything this job does not do, even if it is related to the same people or items. Say yes when one of the listed changes is asked for, even tersely, with filler, or phrased as an offer someone made."}})
        p = check["change"]["noul"]
        if not isinstance(p, (int, float)):
            return {"serve": READONLY}
        if p < 0.5:
            return {"serve": READONLY}
    except Exception:
        return {"serve": READONLY}
    try:
        found = await kit.recall(request, 12)
    except Exception:
        return {"serve": READONLY}
